Theorem
-------

Let a,b,c,e be distinct nonidentity involutions of G satisfying
[a,c]=[a,e]=[b,e]=1. If each of

  L=<a,b,c>, R=<b,c,e>

contains an element of exact order37, the ordered quartet fails the
intersection property.

Proof. Suppose first that L=G. Taking mark sets I={0,1,2} and J={3},
their generated subgroups intersect in <e>, whereas the subgroup on
I intersection J is trivial. Since e is nonidentity, the required
intersection equality fails. The argument for R=G uses I={1,2,3},
J={0} and a instead.

It remains to consider two proper ends. The retained complete maximal
overgroup theorem says that every proper subgroup of this natural G
containing order37 lies in a unitary line stabilizer SU3(11):2. Each
end therefore preserves its specified rational line. The retained and
independently reviewed theorem on two proper order37 ends then applies:
L intersection R strictly contains <b,c>. This contradicts the rank-four
intersection equality for I={0,1,2}, J={1,2,3}. These exhaust the cases.

In particular, the certificate for this sufficient obstruction can consist
of two actual same-end words of exact order37. It does not need a cyclic
span, a fixed vector, a moved-line minor, or a proof of which of the two
proper-versus-full alternatives occurs. Missing words remain inconclusive.

